-### [EDGS](8103-edgs.md)
-Related acceleration methods for 3D Gaussian Splatting.
+A fast ray-tracing method for 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) designed to close the performance gap between ray tracing and rasterization by leveraging the algorithmic advantage of ray tracing: it evaluates only intersected Gaussians, allowing logarithmic scaling in dense scenes with tiny Gaussians. Unlike 3DGS rasterization which operates on tiles and evaluates all Gaussians overlapping a tile for every pixel (linear scaling), GRay uses a Bounding Volume Hierarchy (BVH) to select and evaluate exactly the set of Gaussians intersecting each pixel. It builds on [3D Gaussian Ray Tracing](8101-3d-gaussian-ray-tracing.md), [EPBRR](8102-epbrr.md), and [EDGS](8103-edgs.md).
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Logarithmic scaling with respect to the number of Gaussians in dense scenes, $O(\log N)$ per ray, compared to linear $O(N)$ for rasterization when tiles contain many splats [S1, unofficial][S4].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the BVH structure. Texture-based implementations store BVH nodes and Gaussian proxies in large 2D textures (e.g., 4096x4096 for BVH, 2048x2048 for triangles) [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** On tested hardware, 3DGRT was initially ~9× slower than a recent version of 3DGS rasterization [S4]. However, it avoids the linear scaling penalty of rasterization in highly dense scenes with tiny Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Correct handling of semi-transparency and depth ordering without sorting; efficient culling of non-intersected Gaussians; suitable for hardware-accelerated ray tracing (e.g., OptiX) [S1, unofficial][S2, S4].
-*   **Limitations:** Higher overhead per ray compared to rasterization for sparse scenes; precision issues in BVH traversal due to floating-point comparisons; computational complexity for online visibility querying in dynamic scenes [S2][S3, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Dense scenes with many small Gaussians; scenes requiring physically plausible transparency; hardware with efficient ray-tracing cores.
-*   **When not to use:** Sparse scenes where rasterization overhead is lower; dynamic scenes requiring frequent BVH rebuilds without pre-computation [S2].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Logarithmic scaling in dense scenes with tiny Gaussians due to BVH traversal, compared to linear scaling in rasterization [S1, unofficial]. However, on tested hardware, 3DGRT optimizes about 9× slower than a recent version of 3DGS [S4].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on BVH size. Textures are $4096 \times 4096$ for AABBs and $2048 \times 2048$ for triangles [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Correct handling of semi-transparent particles, shadows, and complex lighting; efficient culling of non-intersected Gaussians [S1, unofficial][S2, S4].
+*   **Limitations:** Slower than rasterization on current hardware; computational complexity of online visibility querying [S2, S4].
+*   **When to use:** Scenes requiring physically accurate transparency, shadows, or relighting [S2].
+*   **When not to use:** Real-time applications where rasterization speed is critical and visual fidelity of transparency is less important [S4].
 
 ## Background
-3D Gaussian Ray Tracing was first studied by Moenne-Loccoz et al. [2024] as an alternative for rendering 3D Gaussians using hardware-accelerated ray tracing (e.g., OptiX) [S4]. It builds on earlier work in point-based ray tracing and BVH construction for real-time rendering [S2]. The method addresses the limitations of rasterization-based 3D Gaussian Splatting, particularly regarding transparency artifacts and scaling in dense environments [S1, unofficial][S4].
+3D Gaussian Ray Tracing was first studied by Moenne-Loccoz et al. [2024] as an alternative for rendering 3D Gaussians using hardware-accelerated ray tracing with OptiX [S4][S1, unofficial]. It addresses the limitations of rasterization-based splatting, such as incorrect alpha blending and lack of shadow casting, by treating Gaussians as semi-transparent particles [S1, unofficial]. The method builds on earlier work in point-based ray tracing and has been further improved by subsequent methods like EPBRR and GRay [S1, unofficial][S4].
 
